# Project Enhancements: Advanced Hero Scroll

I have significantly improved the scroll animations to ensure they are visible and provide a "premium" feel. 

## Key Improvements

1.  **Scrollable Page Content**: Added a "Features" section below the Hero. Previously, the page had no content to scroll to, which made scroll-based animations impossible to see.
2.  **Dramatic Background Reaction**: As you scroll, the background orbs now **increase in blur** and **decrease in opacity**, naturally transitioning into the next section.
3.  **Content Fade & Lift**: The Hero title and buttons now **fade out and move upward** as you scroll away, creating a smooth and modern entrance for the rest of the page.

## Technical Details

````carousel
```tsx
// Scroll Progress Calculation
const handleScroll = () => {
    const scrollY = window.scrollY;
    const viewportHeight = window.innerHeight;
    const progress = Math.min(scrollY / viewportHeight, 1);
    
    // Set CSS variables for background
    container.style.setProperty('--scroll-progress', `${progress}`);
};
```
<!-- slide -->
```css
/* Dynamic Blur & Opacity */
.orb {
    filter: blur(calc(80px + var(--scroll-progress, 0) * 100px));
    opacity: calc(1 - var(--scroll-progress, 0) * 0.5);
    transition: filter 0.2s ease;
}
```
<!-- slide -->
```tsx
// Content Layer Animation
content.style.opacity = `${1 - progress}`;
content.style.transform = `translateY(${progress * -50}px) scale(${1 - progress * 0.05})`;
```
````

## How to Test

1.  Open the page in your browser.
2.  **Scroll down**: Notice how the Hero content gently disappears while the background blurs out to make way for the "Features" section.
3.  **Scroll up**: Watch the Hero section cleanly re-appear.

> [!TIP]
> The scroll effect is now tied to the first 50-100% of the viewport height, making it very responsive to even small scrolls.
